note bottom of WAF
  Blocks bad requests before they reach code.
end note
note bottom of VercelBuild
  Automatic build from the GitHub webhook.
end note
note bottom of TraceCtx
  One trace ID spans middleware, app, and DB.
end note
note bottom of UptimeStore
  Two independent watchers reduce a single point of failure.
end note
note bottom of Runbook
  Human-approved rollback; never fully automatic.
end note
note bottom of DNS
  No custom domain: Cloudflare DNS / Tunnel / Access are not used.
  The Cloudflare free account is only an optional Workers + Queues relay.
end note
note bottom of SentryAlert
  * Developer (free) plan: email alerts are confirmed; webhook / integrations
  must be verified after the 14-day Business trial ends.
  Fallback: email → small parser on EliteDesk → Telegram.
end note
note bottom of MuxTransport
  Strict rules live in the matcher, not beforeSend (matcher runs after beforeSend).
  Send traces / replays to Sentry only; Bugsink accepts errors only.
end note
note bottom of Funnel
  Needs MagicDNS + HTTPS certs + "funnel" nodeAttr. Ports 443 / 8443 / 10000 only.
  Non-configurable bandwidth cap; no auth layer. A port is Serve (private) or Funnel (public).
  Test from mobile data: a tailnet device reaches the host directly, not via the relay.
end note
note bottom of Bugsink
  Independent demo backend; loose rules; no tracing or replay.
  Retention is count-based (no 30-day cutoff). Alert channels on self-hosted: verify.
  BASE_URL = public Funnel URL; check allowed-hosts / CSRF for the :8443 dashboard.
end note
note bottom of CompatGate
  Roll back code safely only while the old version can still read/write
  the expanded schema. Never deploy destructive DDL with its first use.
end note

legend right
  <b>Reading order</b>
  1. Request: People → Edge → Runtime → Cache / Data
  2. Delivery is above; telemetry is below the runtime
  3. Operations and dashboards are the right-side outcomes
  4. Free-tier caveats are marked by notes (DNS, Sentry Alert, Funnel, Bugsink, Backup)
  ----
  <b>Line colours</b>
  <color:#2563EB>Blue</color>: request path
  <color:#0F766E>Teal</color>: cache / data path
  <color:#D97706>Amber</color>: delivery / schema evolution
  <color:#7C3AED>Purple</color>: telemetry / tracing
  <color:#BE123C>Wine</color>: security / recovery guardrail
  <color:#DC2626>Red</color>: actionable alert / incident
  Grey: contextual / dashboard relationship
endlegend
@enduml
